---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { freshness } from "../lib/exports/load";
import { INDICATORS } from "../lib/indicators";
import { href } from "../lib/site";

const tables = freshness();
---

<Layout title="Methodology">
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This site shows evidence about Missouri officials, not verdicts. It has no composite "bias score": any
    comparison between officials ranks one measured number, shown with its source, and every official gets the
    same layout whatever their party. Each figure links to the public record it came from.
  </p>

  <section class="card" id="indicators">
    <h2>Ideology indicators</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Each indicator is shown on its own scale. Scales use a neutral violet-to-orange palette; party is shown
      only as a text label.
    </p>
    {Object.entries(INDICATORS).map(([key, m]) => (
      <div class="ind">
        <h3>
          {m.label} <code class="small muted">{key}</code>
        </h3>
        <p>{m.meaning}</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Scale:</strong>{" "}
          {m.low === "%"
            ? `${m.domain[0]}% to ${m.domain[1]}%`
            : `${m.domain[0]} (${m.low}) to ${m.domain[1]} (${m.high})`}
          . <strong>Limits:</strong> {m.limits}
        </p>
        <p class="small muted">Source: {m.source}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="matching">
    <h2>How records are matched to officials</h2>
    <p>
      Many sources (campaign finance, lobbyist reports) identify people by name only. Each matched record
      keeps a<code>match_confidence</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>exact id</strong>: joined on a shared identifier (FEC candidate id, bioguide id).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>exact (MEC crosswalk)</strong>: the Missouri Ethics Commission's own candidate-committee list
        names the committee.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>high</strong>: first and last name both match.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>medium</strong>: surname matches but the first-name signal is weaker (only the first three
        letters, or a surname-first committee name with no other official sharing the surname). These carry a
        <span class="badge medium">possible match</span> badge, and "Hide possible matches" on any profile
        removes them from every table and chart.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="committee-donors">
    <h2>Following committee money one step back</h2>
    <p>
      When a Missouri committee gives to an official, the Missouri Ethics Commission records the giver by name
      only, with no committee ID. To show who funds that committee, its name is matched to the names Missouri
      committees file under, trying in order: a committee ID written into the name; the same name, ignoring
      case, punctuation and spacing; the name with common abbreviations spelled out ("MO" as Missouri, "Assn"
      as Association); and the name without words like PAC, Political Action Committee or Fund (or a short
      name the filer put in parentheses). A committee only counts if it was active within a year of the gift,
      and only when exactly one committee fits. When several do, no link is made.
    </p>
    <p>
      About 68% of the committee-to-committee dollars on record match one Missouri committee this way. Most of
      the rest come from federal and out-of-state PACs, which report to the FEC or another state instead, and
      from groups known by an acronym. Because the match rests on a name, every link carries a
      <span class="badge medium">possible match</span> badge.
    </p>
    <p>
      The trail ends at businesses and other organizations, nonprofits included: the Ethics Commission gets
      donor reports from committees, not from the organizations that give to them, so where an organization's
      own money came from isn't in these records. Individuals are summed into one row rather than listed by
      name.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="coverage">
    <h2>Coverage and gaps</h2>
    <p>Each profile opens with a coverage strip. The states mean different things:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>● available</strong>: records were found and are shown.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>○ none found</strong>: we looked in a source that covers this office and found nothing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>⊘ not public</strong>: the records exist but aren't published online (for example, Missouri
        personal financial disclosures need a Sunshine Law request). Absence here says nothing about the
        official.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>– n/a</strong>: the measure doesn't apply to this office (for example, judges don't cast
        roll-call votes).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="courts">
    <h2>Court records</h2>
    <p>
      Cases come from CourtListener: published opinions and dockets that name the official as the judge on the
      panel or as an attorney. CourtListener covers appellate and federal courts well and most Missouri trial
      courts barely, so a circuit judge with few cases here may simply sit in a court it doesn't index.
    </p>
    <p>
      Profiles count cases per year and list the most recent ones with the court's own disposition text. They
      do not tally outcomes (affirmed, reversed, who won): turning dispositions into win/loss figures needs a
      reviewed method, and raw rates mislead when judges hear different kinds of cases.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="attendance">
    <h2>Attendance</h2>
    <p>
      Attendance is the share of roll-call votes on which the official was listed, per chamber and session,
      where they voted yea, nay or present. Every other recorded position counts as not attended. The profile
      chart's "Not attended" view shows the other side of the same figure, as a share of recorded votes: one
      bar per session, made of three stacked parts:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Absent or not voting</strong>: marked absent (MO House and Senate) or not voting (Congress).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Absent with leave</strong>: an excused absence, as recorded in the MO Senate journals.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Recused</strong>: the MO Senate's "Excused from voting", a Senate Rule 91 recusal by a member
        who is on the floor. It is not an absence, but it is not counted as attended either, so it lowers the
        attendance figure.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Only the MO Senate records leave and recusals. The MO House and Congress sources don't, so a 0 in those
      bars does not mean the official was never excused: an excused House absence shows up as plain absent.
      The chamber average tick is 100 minus the whole chamber's attendance for that session (every member who
      voted that session, not only the officials this site covers). It lumps all non-attended positions
      together, because the export doesn't split out the chamber's excused absences or recusals, so compare it
      with the end of the official's whole bar, not with one part alone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="pay-per-vote">
    <h2>Pay per vote attended</h2>
    <p>
      Under the attendance chart, each official's pay is divided by the votes they attended, for each calendar
      year and for the years together. Both halves are counted over the same calendar year, using the date of
      each vote, so a year's regular and extraordinary sessions are one row and a Congress is split across its
      two years.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Votes attended</strong>: recorded votes where the official voted yea, nay or present, the same
        count attendance is based on. Absences, "not voting" and Senate Rule 91 recusals are not attended, so
        a recusal does not count here (whether it should is an open question).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pay</strong>, for Missouri officials: gross pay from the state payroll (data.mo.gov, 2017
        onward), meaning everything the payroll shows as paid in that calendar year, not the base salary.
        Several payroll rows in a year, such as a member and a leadership title, are added together. Names on
        the payroll are matched to officials, and unmatched or ambiguous rows are left out. A year whose pay
        rests on a name-only match is flagged "possible match", and hiding possible matches also drops it from
        the total. For members of Congress it is the statutory annual salary of the office, because federal
        pay is not published per person; the year still in progress is left out until it ends, and a member
        who joined mid-year is shown with the full annual salary.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Totals</strong>: the sum of pay over the years that have both pay and votes, divided by the
        votes attended in those same years. A year with votes but no pay counted (all years before 2017, a
        year the payroll has not published yet, a federal year still in progress, or an official whose payroll
        rows could not be matched) is shown but left out of the total, and the note under the table says how
        many years that left out.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A dash</strong> means there is no figure: no pay counted, or no attended vote that year.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The figure divides a whole year's pay by roll-call votes only. It is not an hourly rate or a price per
      day of work: members also do committee work and constituent work that no vote records, and a chamber
      with more roll calls in a year gives a smaller number. A Missouri legislator who started or left
      mid-year has only the pay actually received, so a partial year can look high. For Congress a partial
      year counts the full annual salary against only the votes cast, which also inflates the figure. Compare
      it between officials and years with that in mind.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="awards">
    <h2>Awards</h2>
    <p>
      Each award ranks one measured number, among officials holding the same office, and comes with its
      opposite (lowest and highest attendance, and so on). Ranks use the unrounded figure, so two officials
      shown at the same rounded percentage can hold different ranks; only exact ties share a rank (this holds
      for every award, including the lowest share of money from PACs, committees and parties). The top five
      qualify unless an award sets its own cutoff (the awards_threshold setting). For the attendance and
      party-unity pairs, which rank one number over one field, each end's cutoff is at most half the field
      (ties at the cutoff can add more), and an official tied into both lists is left out of both, so nobody
      holds both ends of a pair in one period. An office needs at least five qualifying officials in a period,
      so single-seat offices like governor have no awards. An official who qualified for the same award in
      consecutive years gets a marker such as X3 (3 qualifying years in a row). Years the official wasn't
      ranked for that award (in another office, not eligible for the measure, or too few officials to rank)
      are skipped: they neither add to nor break the run, so the years in a run can span more calendar years
      than the number shown. A year ranked outside the cutoff breaks it. Lifetime rankings have no marker.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Attendance</strong>: share of recorded votes where the official voted yea, nay or present; at
        least 100 recorded votes in the period.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Money from PACs, committees and parties</strong>: dollars received from them, and (the
        opposite) the lowest share of all money raised, among officials raising at least $10,000. The Missouri
        Ethics Commission files PACs as committees, so the two are counted together. Name-only matches are
        left out.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Party loyalty</strong>: party unity (the share of party-line votes cast with the official's
        own party) in the official's current chamber, per calendar year and over all loaded votes, with the
        number of party-line votes it rests on shown beside it. Needs at least 20 party-line votes in the
        period (the awards_min_party_line_votes setting, default 20). Maverick needs at least one vote against
        the party. The lifetime figure is the same number the profile page shows; yearly figures appear with
        the award only.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="interest-votes">
    <h2>Interests and votes</h2>
    <p>
      Where the site shows money or gifts from an interest next to votes on related bills, that is
      <strong>co-occurrence, not causation</strong>. A donation alongside a vote doesn't show the vote was
      bought, and officials often get money from groups that already agree with them.
    </p>
    <p>
      How the match is made: donors and lobbyist principals are assigned to an industry by name keywords, and
      each industry is tied to bill subject terms from the House subject index and Senate topics. A vote
      appears when the bill carries one of those terms and the industry gave the official money in or before
      that year. Money shown is the total through the latest such vote. Keyword matching misses donors whose
      names don't say what they do, and many large PACs (leadership or pass-through PACs) belong to no single
      industry. <a href={href("/pacs/")}>How PACs move money</a> explains how money can pass through PACs and
      nonprofits before it reaches a candidate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="district-businesses">
    <h2>Businesses in their area</h2>
    <p>
      The businesses come from EPA's Facility Registry Service: every site EPA or a state environmental
      program regulates, with its address, coordinates, industry code and owner. Each site is placed in the
      state House and Senate district, U.S. House district and city that contain it. It is the only free,
      named and geocoded list of Missouri businesses we found, but it covers regulated sites only (factories,
      fuel stations, utilities, repair shops, farms with permits), so offices, stores and most service
      businesses are missing. About a third of the sites have no coordinates and so no district. The industry
      is the site's NAICS code or, when it has none, the NAICS sector its older SIC code maps to; about 4 in
      10 sites have neither.
    </p>
    <p>
      A site is tied to a donor when a non-individual contributor's name equals the site's, its owner's or its
      parent company's after dropping case, punctuation, a leading "the" and words like Inc, LLC and Corp, or
      when both names carry a brand from a short hand-checked list of large companies (so "Ameren Missouri
      PAC" ties to a "UNION ELECTRIC CO" substation). A company that gives under another name is missed, and
      the amount shown is everything those donors gave the official, not money tied to one site. A company
      giving to the official for the area it operates in is <strong>co-occurrence, not influence</strong>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="freshness">
    <h2>Sources and data freshness</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Dataset</th>
            <th>Exported</th>
            <th>Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {tables.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <code>{t.table}</code>
              </td>
              <td>{t.exported_at?.slice(0, 10) ?? "—"}</td>
              <td>
                {t.sample ? <span class="badge medium">sample data (model not built yet)</span> : "real"}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">
      Sources include the Missouri Ethics Commission, Missouri House and Senate journals, Open States, the
      FEC, Voteview, Shor-McCarty, U.S. House and Senate financial disclosures, CourtListener and the Federal
      Judicial Center. The repository's docs/sources.md has the per-source details and known quirks.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  h1 {
    margin-top: 24px;
  }
  /* all prose: keep each card as wide as its text (75ch plus padding) so wide screens don't leave an empty
     right half inside every card */
  .card {
    max-width: calc(75ch + 42px);
  }
  .ind + .ind {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }
</style>
